# LLM-touching code

**One seam:**

```ts
interface ChatClient {
  readonly model: string;
  complete(prompt: string, options?: ChatOptions): Promise<ChatResult>;
}
```

Do not add a second way to reach a model. `analyzer`, `renderer`, `skill` and `patcher`
**do not depend on `@handbook/llm` at all**, and that boundary is what makes `render`,
`skill`, `validate`, `apply` and `rollback` free to run in CI.

**Degrade, never block.** Organization falls back to call-graph order; narration to the
stage description; register extraction to an empty list. A card that could not be written
is **empty** and listed in `_coverage.json` — never dropped, never invented.

**Keep the evidence.** A reply that produced nothing usable is written to
`phase2/cards/_rejected/` (capped, hash-named), so a shape mismatch or a refusal can be
read after the run instead of guessed at.

**A run that gave up exits non-zero.** Returning an apology as a result reports an
abandoned run as a success, and something downstream acts on it.

**Cancellation is cooperative and real.** Thread the `AbortSignal` all the way into the
LLM call. Cancel means the in-flight request aborts, not "stop showing me the log".

**Cache keys cover model, prompt and options.** An empty reply is never cached — a blank
response pinned under a stable key poisons every future run.
